  "explain": [
    {
      "t": "intro",
      "title": "¿Qué son las contracciones?",
      "title_en": "What are contractions?",
      "icon": "🔎",
      "es": "Una contracción junta <b>dos palabras en una</b> y el apóstrofo (') ocupa el sitio de lo que desaparece: I <s>a</s>m → <b>I'm</b>, do n<s>o</s>t → <b>don't</b>. En español tenemos algo parecido («a + el» = al), pero en inglés hay muchísimas. Al hablar, <mark>los americanos contraen casi siempre</mark>: si dices «I am not hungry» palabra a palabra, suenas a robot… o a alguien enfadado. Lo importante es cómo <b>suenan</b>: muchas tienen una sola sílaba.",
      "en": "Contractions join two words into one. Americans use them almost all the time when they speak. Without them, you sound robotic or angry.",
      "formula": [
        "I + am → *I'm* = aim",
        "do + not → *don't* = dount"
      ]
    },
    {
      "t": "rule",
      "num": 1,
      "title": "1 · Con BE: I'm, you're, we're…",
      "title_en": "With BE",
      "icon": "🙋",
      "es": "Am, is y are se pegan al sujeto y se quedan en una sola sílaba: I'm <code>aim</code>, you're <code>iur</code>, he's <code>jiis</code>, she's <code>shiis</code>, it's <code>its</code>, we're <code>wir</code>, they're <code>der</code>. Ojo: you're suena igual que your, y they're igual que there y their (mapa P-05).",
      "en": "Am, is and are stick to the subject: I'm, you're, he's, it's, we're, they're. Most are one syllable.",
      "examples": [
        {
          "en": "I'm hungry!",
          "say": "aim jángri",
          "es": "¡Tengo hambre!",
          "icon": "🍔"
        },
        {
          "en": "We're late!",
          "say": "wir léit",
          "es": "¡Llegamos tarde!",
          "icon": "⏰"
        },
        {
          "en": "They're from Texas.",
          "say": "der frəm téksəs",
          "es": "Son de Texas.",
          "icon": "🤠"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "t": "rule",
      "num": 2,
      "title": "2 · Con NOT: -n't",
      "title_en": "With NOT",
      "icon": "🙅",
      "es": "Not se convierte en <b>-n't</b>. Algunas se quedan en una sílaba (don't <code>dount</code>, can't <code>kænt</code>, aren't <code>arnt</code>) y otras tienen dos, con una ə rapidísima: isn't <code>ísənt</code>, doesn't <code>dásənt</code>, didn't <code>dídənt</code>. La t final apenas se oye (mapa C-03). Can't, a fondo, en el mapa H-08.",
      "en": "Not becomes -n't: don't, can't, isn't, doesn't, didn't. The final t is very soft.",
      "examples": [
        {
          "en": "I don't like broccoli.",
          "say": "ai dount láik brákəli",
          "es": "No me gusta el brócoli.",
          "icon": "🥦"
        },
        {
          "en": "He doesn't eat meat.",
          "say": "ji dásənt íit míit",
          "es": "No come carne.",
          "icon": "🥩"
        },
        {
          "en": "We didn't see it.",
          "say": "wi dídənt síi-it",
          "es": "No lo vimos.",
          "icon": "🙈"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "t": "rule",
      "num": 3,
      "title": "3 · WON'T no es WANT",
      "title_en": "WON'T is not WANT",
      "icon": "⚠️",
      "es": "<b>Won't</b> es will not (no lo haré) y suena <code>wount</code>, con la «ou» de go, igual que don't. <b>Want</b> es querer y suena <code>want</code>, con una a. <mark>Si los confundes, dices lo contrario</mark>: «I won't go» (no iré) frente a «I want to go» (quiero ir).",
      "en": "Won't (= will not) sounds like «wount». Want sounds like «want». Mixing them changes the meaning completely!",
      "formula": [
        "will + not → *won't* = w*ou*nt",
        "want = w*a*nt"
      ],
      "examples": [
        {
          "en": "I won't go.",
          "say": "ai wount góu",
          "es": "No iré.",
          "icon": "🙅"
        },
        {
          "en": "I want to go.",
          "say": "ai wánə góu",
          "es": "Quiero ir.",
          "icon": "🙋"
        },
        {
          "en": "It won't work.",
          "say": "it wount wérk",
          "es": "No va a funcionar.",
          "icon": "🔧"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "t": "rule",
      "num": 4,
      "title": "4 · WILL y WOULD: 'll y 'd",
      "title_en": "WILL and WOULD",
      "icon": "🔮",
      "es": "Will se queda en <b>'ll</b> y would (o had) en <b>'d</b>: I'll <code>ail</code>, you'll <code>iul</code>, we'll <code>wiil</code>, it'll <code>írəl</code>, I'd <code>aid</code>. La L final es oscura, como en ball (mapa C-12). Curiosidad: I'll suena igual que aisle (pasillo).",
      "en": "Will becomes 'll and would (or had) becomes 'd: I'll, we'll, it'll, I'd.",
      "examples": [
        {
          "en": "I'll call you later.",
          "say": "ail kól iu léirer",
          "es": "Te llamo luego.",
          "icon": "📞"
        },
        {
          "en": "We'll see.",
          "say": "wiil síi",
          "es": "Ya veremos.",
          "icon": "🤔"
        },
        {
          "en": "I'd like some water.",
          "say": "aid láik səm wárer",
          "es": "Querría un poco de agua.",
          "icon": "💧"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "t": "rule",
      "num": 5,
      "title": "5 · Should've, could've, would've",
      "title_en": "Should've, could've, would've",
      "icon": "😢",
      "es": "Should have, could have y would have se contraen en <b>should've</b>, <b>could've</b> y <b>would've</b>. La d queda entre vocales y hace el toque (C-02): <code>shúrəv</code>, <code>kúrəv</code>, <code>wúrəv</code>. Deprisa, la v desaparece: <b>shoulda, coulda, woulda</b>. ¡Suena tanto a «should of» que hasta los americanos lo escriben mal!",
      "en": "Should've, could've and would've sound like «shúrəv», «kúrəv», «wúrəv». Fast: shoulda, coulda, woulda.",
      "examples": [
        {
          "en": "I should've called.",
          "say": "ai shúrəv kóld",
          "es": "Debería haber llamado.",
          "icon": "📵"
        },
        {
          "en": "You could've won!",
          "say": "iu kúrəv wán",
          "es": "¡Podrías haber ganado!",
          "icon": "🥈"
        },
        {
          "en": "I would've helped.",
          "say": "ai wúrəv jélpt",
          "es": "Habría ayudado.",
          "icon": "🤝"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "t": "rule",
      "num": 6,
      "title": "6 · Cuándo NO contraer",
      "title_en": "When NOT to contract",
      "icon": "✋",
      "es": "Al <b>final de una respuesta corta</b> afirmativa no se contrae: Yes, I <b>am</b> / Yes, it <b>is</b> (nunca «Yes, I'm»). Y cuando quieres <b>insistir</b>, dices la forma completa con fuerza: I <b>am</b> listening! (¡que sí te escucho!). En los negativos sí puedes: No, I'm not.",
      "en": "Don't contract at the end of a short answer (Yes, I am) or when you want to insist (I AM listening!).",
      "examples": [
        {
          "en": "Are you tired? Yes, I am.",
          "say": "ar iə táierd? iés, ai ǽm",
          "es": "¿Estás cansado? Sí.",
          "icon": "😴"
        },
        {
          "en": "I am listening!",
          "say": "ai ǽm lísəning",
          "es": "¡Que sí te escucho!",
          "icon": "👂"
        },
        {
          "en": "Is it ready? Yes, it is.",
          "say": "ísit réri? iés, it ís",
          "es": "¿Está listo? Sí.",
          "icon": "✅"
        }
      ]
    },
